  "language": "en",
  "question": {
    "prompt": "Positive numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) satisfy \\(xy=16\\). What is the minimum value of \\(x+y\\)?"
  },
  "schemaVersion": "3.0",
  "solutions": [
    {
      "directNodeIds": [
        "cnm_h022"
      ],
      "explanation": "\\(x+y\\ge 2\\sqrt{xy}=8\\), with equality attained when \\(x=y=4\\).",
      "id": "am-gm",
      "sourceRef": "cn-math/cnm_h022_q2#am-gm",
      "title": "AM-GM with a fixed product"
    },
    {
      "directNodeIds": [
        "cnm_148",
        "cnm_h018"
      ],
      "explanation": "The product is fixed, so compare the square of the sum with four times the product. Since \\((x-y)^2\\ge0\\),\n\\[\n(x+y)^2=(x-y)^2+4xy\\ge64.\n\\]\nBecause \\(x+y>0\\), this gives \\(x+y\\ge8\\). Equality occurs at \\(x=y=4\\), which satisfies \\(xy=16\\), so the minimum is \\(8\\).",
      "id": "difference-square",
      "sourceRef": "cn-math/cnm_h022_q2#difference-square",
      "title": "Use a nonnegative difference square"
    },
